  {
   "id": "crush-syntax-md-1",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "# Syntax and Grammar\n\nThis chapter covers the lexical structure and grammar that the Crush parser\n(`crush-frontend`) accepts today. Every `crush` example in this guide is run\nthrough the real toolchain by `scripts/check-examples.py`; where something is\ndescribed in the design but the parser does not accept it yet, the page says so\nand links the crush-ast ticket.\n\n> **Status: alpha.** The language is small and still moving. If a feature isn't\n> in this chapter, assume it doesn't exist yet.\n\n## A program\n\nA Crush file is a sequence of statements. There are two styles, and they can be\nmixed:\n\n- **Script style** — bare top-level statements run top to bottom.\n- **`fn main()`** — an explicit entry function.\n\nWhen a file has both, the top-level statements run first and then `main`'s body\nruns.\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-syntax-1",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "print(\"top-level runs first\")\n\nfn main() {\n    print(\"then main\")\n}\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "runnable"
    ],
    "extra": {
     "guide": {
      "src": "crush/syntax.md:23",
      "browser": "run",
      "expect_output": "top-level runs first\nthen main\n"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-syntax-md-3",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "Functions can be called before they are defined:\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-syntax-2",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "print(seven())\n\nfn seven() {\n    return 7\n}\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
